## The Bug

The problem is a classic Go closure-over-loop-variable issue. In Go 1.21, the loop variable `i` is a **single variable** shared across all iterations. Your goroutines don't capture a *copy* of `i`—they capture a reference to that one variable. By the time any goroutine actually runs, the `for` loop has already completed and `i` has been incremented to `5`. So every goroutine prints `5`.

The specific line is:

```go
fmt.Println(i)  // reads the shared loop variable, which is now 5
```

## The Fix

Pass `i` as an argument to the goroutine function. This creates a new local parameter `j` for each invocation, snapshotting the current value:

```go
go func(j int) {
    defer wg.Done()
    fmt.Println(j)
}(i)
```

<solution language="go" verdict="fix">
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sync"
)

func main() {
	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
		wg.Add(1)
		go func(j int) {
			defer wg.Done()
			fmt.Println(j)
		}(i)
	}
	wg.Wait()
}
</solution>

> **Note:** Starting with Go 1.22, the language spec was changed so that each loop iteration gets its own variable, so your original code would work correctly on 1.22+. But since you're on 1.21, the parameter-passing fix (or equivalently `i := i` at the top of the loop body) is required.

